ABSTRACT: It introduces a multi-agent cybersecurity framework driven by Large Language Models (LLMs). Instead of just theoretical analysis, the setup directly integrates standard industry tools like Nmap, Gobuster, Ffuf and Sqlmap for actual vulnerability scanning. The architecture relies on a three-part agent system. Initially, a Task Planning Agent is responsible for taking a general goal and breaking it down into a list of specific scans to perform. Second, an Execution Agent is in charge of executing these commands in a Windows and WSL environment in a secure manner. Finally, a Result Analysis Agent is responsible for parsing the output from the tools to decide what the next logical step is. To make sure the system is not only effective but also safe and ethical, we have implemented a set of rules for scope enforcement. There is also a use of retry logic, detailed logs, and a JavaScript Object Notation (JSON)-based report format so the decisions made by the agent are easy to follow and understand. This, in essence, gives us a scalable solution for penetration testing

