ABSTRACT: Automating cyberattack simulations is useful for evaluating the cyber resilience of mission-critical infrastructures, such as Tactical Mobile Ad-Hoc Networks (MANETs). Traditional red teaming tools lack adaptability, integration, and scalability, which limits their applicability in dynamic military environments. This paper presents an agentic generative AI system combining Cybersecurity Knowledge Graphs (CSKGs) and Large Language Model (LLM) agents. This system autonomously generates and executes context-aware cyberattack chains. The system analyzes Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI) data, attack tool documentation, and telemetry data to plan and orchestrate multi-stage attacks using tools such as Metasploit and Sliver within an emulated environment. This approach aligns with the Software-defined Defence (SDD) paradigm by enabling software-driven, mission-adaptive simulation of Advanced Persistent Threats (APTs). We evaluate the agent system in a controlled emulated scenario, demonstrating semi-automation from semantic threat representation to system-level exploitation involving human operators.

[R0518] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Smart Systems for Electrical, Electronics, Communication and Computer Engineering (ICSSEECC) | type=CONF
TITLE: LLM-Driven Intelligent Penetration Testing with MCP-Enabled Security Tool Orchestration for Automated Vulnerability Assessment
KEYWORDS: Tools; Artificial intelligence; Modeling; Penetration testing; Security; Printing; Testing; Large language models; Cognition; Cognitive systems; AI-assisted penetration testing; large language models; cybersecurity automation; vulnerability detection; security tool orchestration
ABSTRACT: The rising rate of cybersecurity threats has made traditional, manual penetration testing increasingly cumbersome, resource-demanding, and difficult to perform across complex network structures. This paper presents an AI-driven automated penetration testing framework, Pentest MCP, that combines Large Language Model (LLM) inference with the Model Context Protocol (MCP) to enable intelligent coordination of cyber security tools. The framework lets users trigger security evaluations through natural language instructions, which an LLM-based agent interprets to generate structured penetration-testing workflows. The proposed architecture comprises three layers: an AI reasoning layer, an MCP communication layer, and an execution-and-analysis layer. The reasoning layer performs intent analysis and dynamically selects appropriate security tools; the MCP layer standardizes JSON-RPC communication between the AI agent and external utilities; and the execution layer integrates tools such as Nmap, Nikto, WhatWeb, and DNS-enumeration utilities to automate reconnaissance, service discovery, web-technology fingerprinting, and vulnerability testing. Empirical evaluation in a controlled penetration-testing environment shows the framework effectively detects vulnerabilities such as outdated software, misconfigured services, and denial-of-service vectors, and generates organized reports with severity ratings and remediation guidance. The findings indicate that AI-assisted orchestration substantially improves the efficiency, scalability, and automation of vulnerability-assessment workflows.

[R0519] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Access | type=JOUR
TITLE: The AI-Cybersecurity Nexus: How Large Language Models are Reshaping Threat Intelligence and Digital Defense
KEYWORDS: Computer security; Reviews; Large language models; Bibliometrics; Market research; Ethics; Cyber threat intelligence; Retrieval augmented generation; Intrusion detection; Computer architecture; Cybersecurity; large language models; artificial intelligence; cyber threat intelligence
ABSTRACT: As cybersecurity threats become more sophisticated, the integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into defensive and analytical systems is transforming the field. This paper presents a PRISMA-guided bibliometric and thematic review of 149 studies published between 2015 and 2025, including 117 peer-reviewed journal and conference articles, examining publication trends and dominant research themes in LLM-enabled cybersecurity, organized around five research questions: (i) secure incorporation of LLMs into cyber threat intelligence workflows; (ii) hybrid architectures for privacy-preserving and real-time threat detection; (iii) LLM-enabled secure code remediation; (iv) adversarial misuse and dual-use risks; and (v) multi-layer defense strategies addressing prompt injection, model inversion, and data poisoning. Drawing on over 100 primary studies, the analysis highlights key trends, methodological innovations, and recurring vulnerabilities. Notable developments include decentralized trust-enhanced frameworks, context-aware remediation systems, and simulation-based red teaming. However, gaps persist in adversarial robustness, standardization of evaluation, and ethical governance. By mapping research across technical, operational, and policy dimensions, this review provides a structured basis for advancing trustworthy, resilient, and secure LLM deployments in high-stakes cybersecurity contexts.

[R0520] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE 12th Conference on Big Data Security on Cloud (BigDataSecurity) | type=CONF
TITLE: A Four-Axis Systematization of AI and Cybersecurity in the Era of Large Language Models
KEYWORDS: Artificial intelligence; Computer security; Large language models; Machine learning; Modeling; Printing; Security; Conferences; Machining; Privacy; Artificial Intelligence; Cybersecurity; Large Language Models; AI for CyberSecurity; Cybersecurity for AI; LLMs for Cybersecurity; CyberSecurity for LLMs
ABSTRACT: The convergence of artificial intelligence (AI) and cybersecurity has created a complex ecosystem of capabilities and threats. We systematize this space along four axes: AI-for-Cyber, Cyber-for-AI, LLM-for-Cyber, and Cyber-for-LLMs. Using a PRISMA-guided review of 422 recent works, we develop a unified taxonomy of AI–cyber interactions. AI-for-Cyber enables anomaly detection, intrusion detection, vulnerability analysis, and automated response. Cyber-for-AI highlights threats such as data poisoning, model extraction, and privacy leakage. Cyber-for-LLMs exposes vulnerabilities including prompt injection and jailbreaking, while LLM-for-Cyber introduces reasoning-driven capabilities for threat intelligence and offensive operations. We further identify compound cross-axis threats, where attackers combine techniques across axes (e.g., LLM-generated attacks exploiting AI defenses). This Systematization of Knowledge (SoK) consolidates fragmented literature, highlights systemic risks, and outlines open challenges for securing AI-enabled cyber systems.

