TITLE: Securing Agentic AI: A Comprehensive Threat Model and Mitigation Framework for Generative AI Agents
KEYWORDS: Threat modeling; Generative AI; Prevention and mitigation; Focusing; Computer architecture; Turning; Vectors; Security; Standards; Lenses; enerative AI; threat model; AI agents; cyber-security; attack vectors; security framework
ABSTRACT: As generative AI (GenAI) agents become more common in enterprise settings, they introduce security challenges that differ significantly from those posed by traditional systems. These agents aren’t just LLMs—they reason, remember, and act, often with minimal human oversight. This paper introduces a comprehensive threat model tailored specifically for GenAI agents, focusing on how their autonomy, persistent memory access, complex reasoning, and tool integration create novel risks. This research work identifies 9 primary threats and organizes them across five key domains: cognitive architecture vulnerabilities, temporal persistence threats, operational execution vulnerabilities, trust boundary violations, and governance circumvention. These threats aren’t just theoretical—they bring practical challenges such as delayed exploitability, cross-system propagation, cross system lateral movement, and subtle goal misalignments that are hard to detect with existing frameworks and standard approaches. To help address this, the research work present two complementary frameworks: ATFAA (Advanced Threat Framework for Autonomous AI Agents), which organizes agent-specific risks, and SHIELD, a framework proposing practical mitigation strategies designed to reduce enterprise exposure. While this work builds on existing work in LLM and AI security, the focus is squarely on what makes agents different—and why those differences matter. Ultimately, this research argues that GenAI agents require a new lens for security. If we fail to adapt our threat models and defenses to account for their unique architecture and behavior, we risk turning a powerful new tool into a serious enterprise liability.

[R0522] year=2025 | venue=2025 IEEE Conference on Communications and Network Security (CNS) | type=CONF
TITLE: iThelma: An Autonomous LLM Agent for Cyber Threat Hunting via Playbook-Driven Intelligence
KEYWORDS: Adaptation models; Large language models; Natural languages; Buildings; Reinforcement learning; Network security; Cognition; Human in the loop; Autonomous agents
ABSTRACT: We present an autonomous agent for cyber threat hunting that integrates structured knowledge from human-authored playbooks with the generative and reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs). Building on a prior LLM-based threat-hunting framework, this work introduces additional components for playbook ingestion, hunt script validation, co-occurrence modeling, prompt-based generation, and tuning-based adaptation. The agent generates, validates and refines hunt scripts in a sandboxed environment, identifies behaviorally consistent scripts using consensus voting, and constructs a threat co-occurrence matrix to inform hunt scheduling. Unlike the earlier system that relied solely on natural language prompting, our design enables the agent to learn from execution feedback and adapt its models over time. This work contributes a scalable, self-adaptive and resilient foundation for LLM-driven cyber threat hunting systems.

[R0523] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE Conference on Communications and Network Security (CNS) | type=CONF
TITLE: MaLO: Multi-agent LLM Operations for Autonomous Security
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Security; Complexity theory; Cognition; Cognitive systems; Tools; Large language models; Turning; Architecture; Computer architecture; autonomous cyber operations; multi-agent systems; large language models; security benchmarking; agent topology
ABSTRACT: Despite the wide adoption of Large Language Models (LLMs) and AI agents in cybersecurity, the impact of agent topology on autonomous cyber operations (ACO) performance remains underexplored. Current agent-based security systems rely on either monolithic LLM prompts or rigid, static pipelines. We present MaLO, a configurable security platform to formalize and evaluate dynamic multi-agent graphs. MaLO provides four graph templates unified by a shared “prompt kernel” and tool interface: Single ReAct, Reasoner–Executor, Orchestrator–Workers, and Orchestrator-Workers with a Critic. We evaluate these architectures across a 42-task benchmark of varying difficulty quantified by our new SOCX metric, and a highly realistic, long-horizon security scenario (NSA CBC) that mirrors realworld task ambiguity. We provide the first systematic analysis of agent topology impact on security operations performance. Our results demonstrate that explicit task decomposition and orchestration significantly improve performance on medium and high-complexity security operations. By achieving a 78.6% success rate in our benchmark and solving tasks up to 40x faster than a human expert baseline, we show that architecture selection is a first-class design decision for autonomous cybersecurity agents.

[R0524] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Informatics and Information Technologies (CIIT) | type=CONF
TITLE: Classification and Analysis of Open-Source Gen-AI Penetration Testing Tools
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Tools; Artificial intelligence; Penetration testing; Printing; Machine learning; Computer security; Human in the loop; Security; Servers; cybersecurity; penetration testing; generative ai; model context protocol; retrieval-augmented generation
ABSTRACT: Penetration testing is a complex and resource-intensive process that requires deep technical expertise and extensive tooling. Recent advances in Generative Artificial Intelligence (Gen-AI) and Large Language Models (LLMs), particularly when combined with the Model Context Protocols (MCP), promise to automate penetration testing workflows and lower entry barriers. However, the rapid evolution of this field has created challenges in orientation and assessment. This paper addresses the gap by presenting a systematic methodology for discovering and selecting open-source Gen-AI-based penetration testing tools and classifying them. We conducted a comprehensive GitHub repository search using 14 queries and collected 939 results. By defining and applying rigorous selection criteria, we identified 13 relevant and actively maintained tools. These tools were systematically categorized using a novel categorization framework, covering nine categories: report generation, model support, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) capabilities, state management, interaction models, Penetration Testing Execution Standard (PTES) phase coverage, extensibility, communication interfaces, and deployment models. Our analysis shows that most tools remain in proof-of-concept stages, with MCP wrappers around established penetration testing suites being the most prevalent option. This work provides essential insights for both newcomers and experienced security professionals navigating this emerging field.

[R0525] year=2026 | venue=2026 8th International Congress on Human-Computer Interaction, Optimization and Robotic Applications (ICHORA) | type=CONF
TITLE: Zero-trust Conceptual Architecture for Secure Integration of Large Language Models and Autonomous AI Agents
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Artificial intelligence; Security; Codes; Poles and zeros; Architecture; Computer architecture; Printing; Zero Trust; Large language models; Zero Trust Architecture; Autonomous AI Agents; Large Language Models; Dynamic Trust Evaluation
ABSTRACT: The rapid transition from passive large language models to autonomous agent-based coding systems, including frameworks like Claude Code and OpenClaw, has significantly accelerated software development cycles while simultaneously complicating the enterprise attack surface. Conventional security paradigms relying on perimeter defenses and static role-based access control (RBAC) are inherently designed for deterministic entities, leaving them critically underequipped to mitigate threats posed by non-deterministic algorithmic agents. These autonomous systems frequently generate unpredictable, multi-step sequences of system calls upon ingesting poisoned external environment data or hidden prompt injections. This article proposes a novel Zero Trust architectural framework, complete with rigorous mathematical formalization, specifically engineered for secure multi-agent ecosystems. The architectural core features an intelligent Semantic Gateway deeply integrated with operating system-level coordination modules to dynamically provision ephemeral sandboxing environments. To achieve continuous authorization for asynchronous transactions, the framework utilizes a mathematical structure based on Heterogeneous Graph Neural Networks (HGNN). This algorithm continuously computes an agent's dynamic trust tensor by quantifying the topological divergence between its predicted and actual traversal paths within the deterministic space of a corporate repository graph. To empirically validate the analytical efficacy of this advanced architecture, the research team executed a large-scale simulation processing 100,000 asynchronous transactions. The proposed framework demonstrated exceptional predictive power, achieving a true positive rate (TPR) exceeding 98.7 % in the detection and preventive isolation of critical remote code execution vulnerabilities, including sophisticated CVE-2025-61260 exploits. Crucially, this robust security posture was maintained with a minimal false positive rate (FPR) of 1.1 % and an average processing overhead of just 45 milliseconds. Ultimately, this paradigm modernizes the NIST SP 800-207A standard by replacing static binary access controls with a continuous Markov decision process, transforming stochastic AI unpredictability into a quantifiable engineering asset for critical infrastructures.

[R0526] year=2024 | venue=2024 5th International Conference on Big Data & Artificial Intelligence & Software Engineering (ICBASE) | type=CONF
TITLE: LLMSQLi: A Black-Box Web SQLi Detection Tool Based on Large Language Model
KEYWORDS: Accuracy; Large language models; Closed box; Collaboration; SQL injection; Cognition; Security; Testing; Payloads; Software engineering; SQLi detection; Web Security; Large Language Model; Multi Agents
ABSTRACT: Black box detection tools of SQL injection vulnerabilities simulate real-world web attack scenarios, making it essential for evaluating SQLi risks in actual web applications. However, current black-box approaches depend on predefined rules for SQLi vulnerability detection, which limits both their efficiency and accuracy. In this paper, we propose a black-box SQLi detection tool based on large language multi-agent, LLMSQLi, which uses the context understanding and reasoning capabilities of large language models and the cooperative division of labor mode of multi-agent to generate payloads customized for test targets and efficiently detect SQL injection vulnerabilities in Web programs. Drawing inspiration from real-world teams of security experts, LLMSQLi simulates the step-by-step process of human experts in testing tasks through the LLM Muti-Agent collaboration model. We ran experiments on SQLiMicroBenchmark to compare the performance of LLMSQLi and two of the most advanced black-box SQLi testing tools. Experiments show that LLMSQLi successfully detected all 15 targets and outperformed other tools.

[R0527] year=2025 | venue=2025 IEEE 24th International Conference on Trust, Security and Privacy in Computing and Communications (TrustCom) | type=CONF
TITLE: L2M-AID: Autonomous Cyber-Physical Defense by Fusing Semantic Reasoning of Large Language Models with Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning
KEYWORDS: Translation; Large language models; Semantics; Reinforcement learning; Cyber-physical systems; Stability analysis; Cognition; Security; Time factors; Industrial Internet of Things; cyber-physical systems security; multi-agent reinforcement learning; large language models; autonomous defense; semantic reasoning
ABSTRACT: The increasing integration of Industrial IoT (IIoT) exposes critical cyber-physical systems to sophisticated, multi-stage attacks that elude traditional defenses lacking contextual awareness. This paper introduces L2M-AID, a novel frame-work for Autonomous Industrial Defense using LLM-empowered, Multi-agent reinforcement learning. L2M-AID orchestrates a team of collaborative agents, each driven by a Large Language Model (LLM), to achieve adaptive and resilient security. The core innovation lies in the deep fusion of two AI paradigms: we leverage an LLM as a semantic bridge to translate vast, unstructured telemetry into a rich, contextual state representation, enabling agents to reason about adversary intent rather than merely matching patterns. This semantically-aware state empowers a Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) algorithm, MAPPO, to learn complex cooperative strategies. The MARL reward function is uniquely engineered to balance security objectives (threat neutralization) with operational imperatives, explicitly penalizing actions that disrupt physical process stability. To validate our approach, we conduct extensive experiments on the benchmark SWaT dataset and a novel synthetic dataset generated based on the MITRE ATT&CK for ICS framework. Results demonstrate that L2M-AID significantly outperforms traditional IDS, deep learning anomaly detectors, and single-agent RL baselines across key metrics, achieving a 97.2% detection rate while reducing false positives by over 80% and improving response times by a factor of four. Crucially, it demonstrates superior performance in maintaining physical process stability, presenting a robust new paradigm for securing critical national infrastructure.

[R0528] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE 50th Annual Computers, Software, and Applications Conference (COMPSAC) | type=CONF
TITLE: Neuro-Agentic Control: A Deep Learning-Based LLM-Powered Agentic AI Framework for Controlling Security Controls
KEYWORDS: Modeling; Timing; Long short term memory; Monitoring; Forecasting; Large language models; Foundation models; Printing; Noise; Physics; Agentic AI; Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG); Time series prediction; Foundation models; Gemini 2.5 Flash lite; LSTM; TCN
ABSTRACT: Cyberattacks on operational technology are increasingly causing costly downtime and physical damage, exposing the limitations of traditional rule-based monitoring in industrial IoT environments. While Large Language Models (LLMs) have strong semantic reasoning abilities to assist in decision support, their hallucinatory nature presents unacceptable safety liabilities for closed-loop control. This paper introduces a neuro-agentic control framework, a novel architecture that couples an LLMbased planner (i.e., such as Gemini 2.5 Flash-Lite) with a pretrained Time-Series Foundation Model (TimesFM), to achieve physics-grounded autonomous defense. The paper introduces a “Counterfactual Physics Injection” mechanism that simulates the impact of LLM-proposed interventions within the numerical latent space of the foundation model before actuation, while allowing the system to reject hallucinatory or unsafe actions. Evaluated on an industrial dataset (e.g., the Secure Water Treatment (SWaT)) in the context of stochastic attack scenarios, the framework exhibited better performance compared to LSTM and TCN baselines. The Neuro-Agentic Loop prevented five breaches (33.3%) below the threshold versus LSTM (26.7%) and TCN (13.3%), with zero physically invalid (hallucinated) actions executed. These results demonstrate the efficacy of using foundation models as deterministic “Sentinels” to safeguard agentic AI in critical infrastructure.

[R0529] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Next-Gen Intelligent Computing and Applications (ICONICA) | type=CONF
TITLE: Cyber security Integration Framework using Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning for Agent OS : with Adaptive Threat Defence and Governance Compliance
KEYWORDS: Tools; Security; Artificial intelligence; Modeling; Printing; Memory; Poles and zeros; Computer security; Permission; Large language models; Agentic AI; Agent Operating System; Cybersecurity; Zero Trust; MITRE ATT&CK; STRIDE; Prompt Injection; Privilege Escalation; GRC; NIST AI RMF; Threat Intelligence; Multi-Agent Systems; LLM Security
ABSTRACT: LLM powered autonomous agents do not have principled middleware for lifecycle management, orchestration of multi-agent systems, and risk-tailored cybersecurity. We introduce Agent OS - a five-layer operating system abstraction with an Agent Kernel, Zero-Trust Permission Controller, integrity-checked Memory Management, and an ephemerally-sandboxed Tiered Tool Registry designed to address the agentic cybersecurity domain. The newly introduced Orchestration Layer trains a Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) policy with Contract-Based Compositional Shielding to avoid unsafe agent behaviors, circumventing the brittleness of hierarchical task planning while maintaining theoretical runtime safety guarantees. The Cybersecurity Integration Framework implements a novel approach to the MITRE ATT&CK for AI matrix, provisions Zero Trust Network Access between agents, and utilizes LoRA-adapted Small Language Models for zero-shot adversarial prompt detection with GRC-compliant audit logging capabilities for the EU AI Act and NIST AI RMF frameworks.Our initial implementation demonstrated on a proof-of-concept prototype across four use cases (two of which were adversarial redteam tests) achieved an 84.7% task success rate, reduced cybersecurity violations by 85.7%, decreased successful prompt injection attempts by 79.4% (from 68% to 11% of trials), and achieved a 12% reduction in latency versus uninstrumented baselines. We introduce the first formalism for a cybersecurity architecture in the domain of agentic AI systems and present a potential reference architecture for standardization within the community.

[R0530] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Security & Privacy | type=JOUR
TITLE: From Promise to Peril: Rethinking Cybersecurity Red and Blue Teaming in the Age of LLMs
KEYWORDS: Privacy; Large language models; NIST; Cognition; Computer security
ABSTRACT: This position article maps large language model (LLM) capabilities onto the MITRE ATT&CK and NIST Cybersecurity Frameworks. While LLMs exhibit fluency and versatility across cybersecurity tasks, they remain fragile in complex, high-stakes settings due to limited context retention, hallucinations, and reasoning gaps.

[R0531] year=2026 | venue=2026 8th International Conference on Artificial Intelligence Technologies and Applications (ICAITA) | type=CONF
TITLE: A Workflow-Constrained and Retrieval-Augmented LLM Framework for Automated Penetration Testing
KEYWORDS: Penetration testing; Large language models; Modeling; Modules (abstract algebra); Testing; Retrieval augmented generation; Cognition; Cognitive systems; Tools; Security; Automated Penetration Testing; Large Language Models; DeepSeek-R1; Structured Task Tree; Retrieval-Augmented Generation
ABSTRACT: Large language models (LLMs) have shown practical potential in penetration testing by interpreting tool outputs and generating executable commands. However, in long-horizon workflows, they can lose global context, repeat low-value actions, and rely on incomplete domain knowledge. This paper proposes a workflow-constrained and retrieval-augmented LLM framework for automated penetration testing. The framework keeps the reasoning, generation, and parsing design of prior LLM-based testing systems, but introduces two lightweight mechanisms. A Structured Task Tree (STT) constrains workflow-level reasoning by limiting the next task to valid candidates in a predefined testing process. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) enhances command generation by adding relevant vulnerability knowledge, tool usage records, and historical execution evidence. The parsing module extracts structured evidence from tool outputs and feeds it back to the next reasoning step. Experiments on five VulnHub boot2root targets show that the proposed framework improves the subtask completion rate from 67.6% to 78.9% compared with the baseline workflow.

[R0532] year=2025 | venue=2025 IEEE International Conference on Computing (ICOCO) | type=CONF
TITLE: The cyber kill chain for autonomous AI agents
KEYWORDS: Adaptation models; Feedback loop; Generative AI; Computer architecture; Planning; Control theory; Artificial intelligence; Computer security; Optimization; Next generation networking; adaptive; AI agents; autonomous; cognitive; cybersecurity; cyber kill chain
ABSTRACT: This paper introduces the Autonomous Attack Chain (AAC), a framework designed to conceptualise and model the distinct phases of offensive cyber behaviour executed by artificial intelligence (AI) agents. Optimised for human-led attacks, traditional cyber kill chain frameworks often inadequately capture the dynamic, adaptive, and cognitive capabilities inherent in modern AI systems. The AAC addresses this gap by synthesising foundational theories, including related cognitive architecture models such as Belief-Desire-Intention, Observe–Orient–Decide–Act, Goals-Plans-Action, and the Perceptual Control Theory. Conceptualised as a continuous, cyclical process centred around an AI agent’s Cognitive Core (a generative AI model), the AAC outlines phases such as Planning Initiation, Data/Tool Acquisition, Execution and Adaptation, Persistence/Propagation, and Impact Optimisation. This model explicitly accounts for the agent’s ability to reason over uncertain environments, form internal representations of success, and dynamically adjust actions based on environmental feedback, embodying the adaptive and learning affordances crucial to autonomous cyber operations. Thus, the AAC provides a better understanding of AI-driven threats, laying essential groundwork for developing advanced, adaptive defensive strategies.


